  MIT License

  OpenKaava's GitHub panel is adapted from Orca's, which does the same job in an
  Electron application. What was taken is design rather than text: the data
  model for one list holding issues and pull requests together, the `is:` and
  `label:` filter vocabulary, and the arrangement of a row. Orca's own client
  shells out to the `gh` command line from Node, which has no counterpart in a
  Rust backend, so none of it was translated line for line.

  OpenKaava's diff annotations are adapted from Orca's diff comments. Two pieces:
  the format review notes are serialized into for an agent to read, together
  with the escaping rule that makes that format survive being pasted at a shell
  prompt, from `src/shared/diff-comments-format.ts`; and the review-comment data
  model — how a note anchors to a line range, and which of its timestamps mean
  what — from `src/shared/diff-comment-types.ts`.

  The files carrying a note that names the Orca file they came from:

    src-tauri/src/github.rs
    src/shell/github/query.ts
    src/shell/github/GithubPanel.tsx
    src-tauri/src/review/mod.rs
    src/shell/diff/reviewPrompt.ts

  Nothing of Orca's branding is used, and none of it appears in any user-facing
  string.

