TRADEMARKS

The license covers the code. It does not cover the names.

OpenKaava and Schematify, together with the OpenKaava mark, wordmark and app
icons, are trademarks of Veistra. Apache-2.0 grants copyright and patent
rights and says plainly, in section 6, that it grants no trademark rights. This
section is not an extra restriction bolted on top of the license;
it is a statement of where the line the license already draws actually falls,
written out because a reader should not have to infer it.

So: fork this repository, change it, build a product on it, sell that product,
and say in plain words that your work is based on OpenKaava, or is compatible with
OpenKaava, or is a fork of OpenKaava. All of that is fine and none of it needs
permission. What is not fine is shipping it *as* OpenKaava — under the name, under
the marks, under the wordmark or the app icon, or in any other way that would
leave someone believing they had installed the build Veistra publishes.

The reason is narrow and worth saying out loud, because "we kept the trademark"
usually is not. Once the source is freely copyable, the name is the only thing
left that tells a user which build is in front of them, and this shell is a
program whose entire job is loading and executing other people's tools on that
user's machine. A build that is not ours but answers to our name is the one
confusion that costs somebody something real. Rust, Docker and Mozilla all draw
the same line for the same reason; the alternative — a copyleft license, or no
license at all — would have cost far more and protected less.

A fork should not have to hunt for the marks in order to remove them. Stripping
them is meant to be an edit to the repository's branding configuration rather
than a grep through the source, and if it ever is not, that is a bug worth
reporting.
